@@ -0,0 +1,56 @@
+# ECON-02B2d12 — Safe warehouse decommissioning and demolition
+
+**Date:** November 1, 2026
+**Project:** Single Galaxy / authoritative colony simulation (Linux development)
+**Exact input baseline:** `SingleGalaxy_ECON02B2d11_Warehouse_Salvage_Supply_Fulfillment_Source_Tests_2026-11-01.zip`
+**Status:** Local implementation and Linux tests complete; Windows GUI acceptance not performed. GitHub not updated.
+
+## Objective
+
+Let a colony operator withdraw an installed warehouse from service and demolish it **only after its physical inventory and dependencies are resolved**. Do not create free resources, lose stock, bypass reserved materials, or remove facilities while named NPCs have jobs depending on them. Never use arbitrary world/factory area caps or punish absence.
+
+## Implementation
+
+### Authoritative world state and construction lifecycle
+
+- Three explicit actions added to the existing colony simulation: `MarkStorageNodeDecommission`, `CancelStorageNodeDecommission`, `DemolishStorageNode`. These are bound to the same owner/ship/site range and execution checks as other construction commands.
+- A **v35 snapshot** includes `storage_decommission|site|node` markers, preserving incomplete work across SQLite restarts. v1–v34 snapshots remain accepted. Invalid markers and orphaned storage are rejected during restore.
+- The warehouse remains installed during decommissioning; its generic good stacks, placed position and module stock remain physical and persistent.
+- Decommissioned bins refuse new inbound generic-good transfers, inbound construction-module deposit, new construction reservations, new restock assignments, and deterministic source allocation. Existing unreserved material can still be salvaged by an identified NPC and returned to the depot. Reservations can be released, without moving any inventory unrealistically.
+- Marking a bin is refused when attached to an existing conveyor/production link. No live link is silently broken. A separate authorized link-removal feature is needed before those bins can be demolished.
+- Final demolition requires the decommission marker, an **empty generic stack bin**, no node construction modules, no plan reservations at the node, no attached links, and enough capacity in the local module depot. Its equipment placement and empty bin are removed atomically by the world action. The intact built warehouse's embodied one construction module is recovered into that site's **local** depot; this represents intact disassembly/reuse, **not** an assertion of lossless ore recycling.
+- `DemolishStorageNode` emits a 2 ore-equivalent resource receipt from `warehouse-structure:<site>:<node>` to `construction-depot:<site>`. SQLite's conservation ledger validates the command, event quantity and exact endpoints, and counts the receipt among expected construction transfers. World totals are unchanged.
+
+### NPC and host authority checks
+
+- The colony's `Crews::warehouse_job_dependency` checks the active SQL restock/salvage assignments for the node, plus active construction workers physically at that equipment location. The native colony factory control rejects marker changes and demolition when dependencies exist, on the **same authoritative simulation thread**.
+- No browser-provided player identity is trusted: `WAREHOUSE_MARK`, `WAREHOUSE_CANCEL`, and `WAREHOUSE_DEMOLISH` require a real owned colony site. The existing localhost relay enforces session tokens, same-origin JSON, canonical numeric node IDs and allowlisted operations.
+- The colony UI shows installed node stock, reserved modules, generic goods volume, connected link count, and current decommission state, with separate **Decommission**, **Recommission**, and **Demolish empty node** buttons. Demolition requires an explicit browser confirmation. No auto-demolition policy is enabled.
+- The GUI can show an empty installed bin even if no construction-supply record yet exists; it does not depend on positive stored modules for node discovery.
+
+## Tests performed
+
+| Gate | Result | Evidence |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Full Linux CMake/Ninja native build | **PASS** | All existing native executables and the new d12 test target linked successfully |
+| Non-live native CTest selection | **69/69 PASS** | `ctest -j5 -E 'live\|relay\|HTTP\|http\|native_factory\|integrated'` |
+| Live/relay native CTest selection, excluding one older timing-sensitive HTTP test | **29/29 PASS** | `ctest -j5 -R 'live\|relay\|HTTP\|http\|native_factory\|integrated' -E '^ECON02B2d6_NPCConstruction_HTTP$'` |
+| Previously excluded NPC HTTP test, isolated | **1/1 PASS** | `ctest -R '^ECON02B2d6_NPCConstruction_HTTP$'` |
+| Entire registered native suite (disjoint runs) | **99/99 PASS** | 69 + 29 + 1; not claimed as one uninterrupted run |
+| JavaScript tests | **93/93 PASS** | `node --test tests/*.js` |
+| Offline Chromium colony UI scripts | **2/2 PASS** | Crew and structures browser smoke scripts; d12 crew test clicks decommission, cancel and confirm demolition |
+| New d12 native lifecycle test | **PASS** | Reservations, stock, unauthorized use, connected-link blockers, no-double removal, SQLite restart and conservation |
+| New d12 authenticated HTTP test | **PASS** | Forged token, invalid node/site, cross-origin denial, persistent marking, recommission, demolition and idempotent refusal |
+
+The first combined live/relay run completed 28 of 29 tests before its execution window expired. The remaining legacy NPC HTTP test subsequently passed **alone**, and the other 29 tests were rerun as a successful independent group with that one excluded. No native regressions remain observed in these checks.
+
+## Limitations and next gates
+
+1. Demolition itself is currently an explicitly authorized **single world command**, not a timed NPC crew job. Follow-up work should add a crew with a route, safety exclusion zone, equipment-dismantling time and separate salvage handling, preserving the existing authorization checks.
+2. Installed conveyor links must be dismantled first, but the project does not yet expose a dedicated safe link-decommission command. Such warehouses will remain blocked and safe, not force-removed.
+3. No generalized machine teardown or reuse/damage quality grades, nor arbitrary multi-resource item categories beyond existing node goods, are implemented here.
+4. Local NPC routing is a provisional 2D grid, not production-quality 3D industrial movement.
+5. Windows x86-64 compilation, interactive GUI operation and graphics acceptance have not been verified in this Linux environment.
+6. GitHub is a source repository, not the development execution environment. These changes were developed and tested locally; no GitHub mutation has occurred.
+
+**Recommended next milestone:** ECON-02B2d13 — physically staffed dismantling, safe conveyor unlinking, and deterministic NPC task handoff/evacuation with crash/restart recovery.
